I found comprehensive information about offline-first expense trackers, sync conflict resolution, and database approaches. Here are the key findings:

## Offline-First Architecture for Expense Trackers

A common pattern is: all writes go to local SQLite first with an isSynced flag, and a background sync service watches network connectivity and pushes unsynced records when the network returns.

## Conflict Resolution Strategies

Offline sync architecture relies on local-first data storage, optimistic updates, delta syncing for efficiency, and various conflict-resolution strategies, such as last-write-wins or CRDTs.

For expense tracking specifically, apps dealing with sensitive or complex data like financial transactions can't rely on simple "last-write-wins" strategies, as this could lead to data loss. If you edited the same expense on two devices while both were offline, the app needs rules for which version wins—the standard approach is "last write wins" where the most recent edit takes precedence.

## Lost Updates Problem

Data conflict arises when multiple devices or users attempt to modify the same piece of data concurrently, often in offline-first applications where devices work in isolation before syncing.

## Database Considerations

The pattern uses local SQLite for client-side storage, with backend sync using services like Supabase. Offline-first requires managing local databases, sync engines, and conflict resolution logic.

## Key Design Principles

A key design decision is to never block the user on sync—the app doesn't wait for confirmation from the server, and records should have an updatedAt timestamp so the server can determine conflict ordering.


